# {{ service_title }} — rules for an agent working in this repo

Read this before writing code. The architecture here is **enforced**, not
suggested: `jfast contracts check` fails the build on a violation, so a change
that ignores these rules does not merge.

Full detail lives in `.jfast/skills/`. Load the skill for the task you are
doing rather than reading all of them.

## The shape

```
{{ service_title }}/
├── main.py            entry point; routers are registered here
├── contracts.toml     the layer and module rules, machine-checked; written with the first module
├── jfast.toml         config, and the layout each module was generated with
├── modules/           one folder per business capability
│   └── <name>/
│       ├── public.py  the only file other modules may import; returns DTOs
│       ├── README.md  this module's file map, and what each file may import
│       └── tests/     required: a module with no tests fails the check
└── shared/            vocabulary two or more modules speak: enums, types, pure functions
    └── enums.py
```

## Where a module keeps its files

Inside a module the shape depends on the layout it was generated with, and this
service can hold more than one — that is the point of a modular monolith, and a
capability with real rules earns a different shape from a table with a router
on top:

```bash
jfast new module order                       # modular (default): one folder per layer
jfast new module order --layout layered      # one file per layer
jfast new module order --layout screaming    # one file per use case
jfast new module order --layout hexagonal    # ports and adapters
```

So this file does not name the files inside a module: it was written before any
of them existed. Two things do, and both are written next to the code:

- `jfast.toml`, table `[modules.<name>]` — the layout that module was generated
  with;
- `modules/<name>/README.md` — the file map for that layout, with the role of
  each file and what it may import.

Read both before adding a file to an existing module. Do not copy the shape of
a neighbouring module: they are allowed to differ.

`contracts.toml` is written by the **first** `jfast new module` here, with the
layer paths of that module's layout — a service has no layout of its own, and
guessing one produced a contract that matched nothing and enforced nothing.

That is the cost of the freedom above. A module you add in a *second* layout
matches no layer glob, so only the service-wide rules below reach it and the
check passes without having looked at it. `jfast contracts check` reports a
layer that matched no file at all (`layer-unmatched`), which catches a contract
left behind when a service moves wholesale to another layout; it cannot catch
the mixed case, because the first layout's layers still match their own modules.
So if you add a module in a second layout, add its paths to `contracts.toml`
yourself and say so in the pull request. Nothing else will.

## Six rules that are actually checked

1. **The layer that answers HTTP does not touch the database.** It parses,
   calls the layer below it, and returns. Every layout's contract sets
   `forbid_packages = ["sqlalchemy"]` on that layer, whatever its files are
   called, so the import fails the check rather than the review.
2. **A module talks to another only through its `public.py`.** Need data
   another module owns: call a function in `modules/<other>/public.py` (add it
   there if it is missing: it takes the session and `tenant_id`, returns a DTO)
   and list `<other>` in `depends_on` under `[modules.<yours>]` in
   `contracts.toml`. Need to react to something it did: subscribe to its event
   through the outbox. Anything else of another module is private, and two
   modules that call each other are a cycle the check rejects.
3. **No raw SQL against another module's tables.** It is the same coupling as
   an import with nothing to see it; the check matches table names.
4. **`shared/` never imports a module.** The direction is one-way, or the
   dependency graph becomes a circle and nothing can be extracted later.
5. **`shared/` is vocabulary: nothing in it touches the database.** Two
   modules sharing a repository is two modules sharing a table; behaviour goes
   in the owner's `public.py`, not here.
6. **Enums inherit `str`.** A plain `Enum` serialises as `Status.DRAFT` down
   some paths and `"DRAFT"` down others.

`jfast contracts show --json` lists every layer with the paths it covers. That
is the answer to "which file am I allowed to write this in", and it is derived
from this project rather than from a convention.

Run the checker yourself before claiming a change is done:

```bash
jfast contracts check
```

## Settings the environment owns

Most of `jfast.toml` is the shape of this service, and the file wins over the
environment for it. These keys describe **where the service runs** instead, and
for them the process environment wins: a value `jfast.toml` writes for one is
only the default used where its variable is unset.

{% for section, keys in environment_owned %}
- `{{ section }}` {{ keys }}
{% endfor %}

`jfast ai context` lists, under `environment`, which of them this file writes
and which the current environment overrides; the full table is in the
framework's deploy guide, "Which wins".

When the two disagree -- a boot WARNING `... in jfast.toml is overridden by
JFAST_...`, or a notice in `jfast check` -- **tell the human; do not "fix"
either side.** Editing the file changes nothing wherever the variable is set,
and the environment belongs to whoever deploys the service. Never write a
secret into `jfast.toml` to make a value agree.

## Do not

- **Do not add a dependency** without saying so. Check whether the framework
  already provides it — `jfast add` with no argument shows the catalogue.
- **Do not write a version number into a template or a requirements file.**
  It is derived from the installed framework.
- **Do not invent a new folder** at the top level. If something does not fit
  `modules/` or `shared/`, that is a design question, not a filing question.
- **Do not disable a contract rule to make a check pass.** Waive one line with
  `# contracts: allow <reason>` and say why in the pull request.

## A frontend for this service

A UI is a service of its own, generated beside this one rather than inside it:

```bash
jfast new service admin --kind spa --frontend vue                     # the nexora look
jfast new service admin --kind spa --frontend vue --template classic  # plain Tailwind
```

Two looks ship: `nexora`, the default — liquid glass, an island top bar, a
WebGL ribbon, an accent picker — and `classic`. **If the user asked for a look,
generate that one**; the default is what you use when nobody said. Inside the
frontend, `jfast new view <Name>` draws each new page in the look the project
was generated with.
With `--agent-docs`, a `nexora` frontend also gets the `nexora-reference`
skill: a copy of JFast Suite, the pages the look was drawn from, to look at
before building a screen it does not have yet.

## Commands

| | |
| --- | --- |
| `jfast serve` | run this service locally |
| `jfast exec -- <command>` | alembic, pytest or a script, with `.env` translated for the host |
| `jfast new module <name>` | a new module, wired up |
| `jfast new enum <Name>` | an enum, in one module or in `shared/` |
| `jfast contracts check` | enforce everything above |
| `jfast contracts show --json` | the layers, and which paths each one covers |
| `jfast doctor` | check the config resolves and every plugin imports |
| `jfast describe --json` | the whole service, machine-readable |

`jfast describe --json` is the fastest way to answer "what is in this service"
without reading every file.
